{
  "dom_id": "step-e7903",
  "name": "Example: Solving Inequalities Requiring Multiplication or Division by a Negative Factor",
  "problem": "Solve the inequality\n\n$$\n(2\\sin{x}-9)(2x) \\leq 0.\n$$",
  "worked_solution": "Note that the factor $2\\sin{x}-9$ is always negative. We know this because\n\n$$\n-1 \\leq \\sin x \\leq 1 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad -11 \\leq 2\\sin x - 9 \\leq -7.\n$$\n\nBecause $2\\sin{x}-9$ is always negative, we can divide both sides of the inequality by this quantity, but we have to remember to flip the sign of the inequality. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x-9)(2x) & \\le 0 \\\\ \\frac{(2\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x-9)(2x)}{2\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x-9} & \\ge \\frac{0}{2\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x-9} \\\\ 2x & \\ge 0 \\\\ x & \\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "result": null,
